00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.769
All right, we're back with the continuation of Lab 4.

2
00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.772
Our setup will be identical to the previous notebook.

3
00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:10.477
Across those first four cells, we've imported some general data science libraries,

4
00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:12.879
set up LandingAI's ADE,

5
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.782
loaded the API key from the environment variable,

6
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.584
and we're ready to go.

7
00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:20.387
As before, we'll import some helper functions

8
00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:23.289
which you can examine separately in the helper file.

9
00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:25.792
In exercise three, you'll assume the role

10
00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:27.994
of a person who works at a bank

11
00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:30.397
and is reviewing loan applications.

12
00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:33.400
And we'll develop a fairly realistic

13
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.502
full document processing pipeline.

14
00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:37.604
If you've ever applied for a loan,

15
00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:41.107
you were probably asked to upload various financial documents.

16
00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:44.411
You probably supplied some sort of income statement

17
00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:46.079
such as a pay stub.

18
00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:47.847
some sort of tax form showing

19
00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:49.916
your total income from the year before.

20
00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:52.585
Maybe a bank statement or an investment statement

21
00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:54.487
to show the value of your assets,

22
00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:57.891
and probably also some sort of government-issued ID.

23
00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:01.594
Now, the challenge for the bank receiving all of those documents

24
00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:04.597
is that they may have all sorts of crazy names.

25
00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:08.401
It might be upload A, might be image 456.

26
00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:12.505
They need to understand what is this document that you uploaded,

27
00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:14.774
and then they need to pull out the

28
00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.810
key value pairs that they're actually interested in,

29
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.812
such as that total asset value.

30
00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:21.614
So that's the processing pipeline we're going to build here.

31
00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.050
We're going to have a user

32
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.519
upload multiple mixed documents of unknown types.

33
00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:30.723
We're going to parse all of them, understand what they are,

34
00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:34.861
extract key value pairs from them and then run some verification.

35
00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:37.030
And we're going to do all of

36
00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.065
this using ADE as our main API.

37
00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:42.969
So, let's do it. First, preview the documents that have been supplied.

38
00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:45.438
To do that, we'll create a function

39
00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:47.674
that displays everything in a particular directory.

40
00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:50.443
So before I execute this cell, I'll warn

41
00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:52.879
you that these documents are all from different people.

42
00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:55.381
This is not actually a complete

43
00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:58.751
loan kits because we had to work with sample documents

44
00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.154
that are anonymized from the internet.

45
00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:03.556
So here's upload A PDF,

46
00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:06.059
looks to be a Fidelity investment statement.

47
00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:09.462
upload B looks like a payroll statement.

48
00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:13.266
upload C, first glance looks like a bank statement.

49
00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:15.935
upload D looks like a passport,

50
00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:19.639
and upload E looks like a US W-2 form.

51
00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.408
And of course, I recognize all

52
00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:24.811
of these instantly with my human vision.

53
00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:27.380
But how is a document processing pipeline

54
00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.916
going to understand these in the same way?

55
00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:34.621
Recall that all the documents came in with names such as uploadA.

56
00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:36.823
So we will need to categorize them.

57
00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:40.660
And to do that, we'll be using the extract API from ADE

58
00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.663
with a pydantic JSON schema.

59
00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:46.032
Now, this schema looks a little bit

60
00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:48.168
different than the schema from exercise one,

61
00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:51.371
because we're using pydantic rather than JSON.

62
00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:55.375
These are the two options that ADE accepts as an input.

63
00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:58.311
Here we find a class DocumentType

64
00:02:58.311 --> 00:03:01.114
where we enumerate the five types that we're expecting

65
00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:05.018
and provide a rich description of what each might look like.

66
00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:08.721
And then a class DocType to bring that all together.

67
00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:11.724
Now we'll proceed to again use pydantic

68
00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:15.128
to define document-specific extraction schemas.

69
00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:17.363
What do we want from the pay stub?

70
00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:19.566
What do we want out of the investment statement?

71
00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:21.267
What do we want out of the

72
00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:22.769
bank statement? and so on and so forth.

73
00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:27.540
In this cell, we have five different document-specific Pydantic schemas.

74
00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.742
Let's just take a look at one or two of them.

75
00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:32.345
So, in the class for the IDSchema,

76
00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:35.348
we're expecting some sort of government-issued ID,

77
00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:37.750
such as a driver's license or a passport.

78
00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:40.753
That should definitely contain the full name of the person.

79
00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:45.058
It should have the state or country issuing the identification.

80
00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:49.329
It should have issue_date and some sort of unique identifier.

81
00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:52.131
So these were the four strings that we're looking for

82
00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:55.535
in the field extraction step from the ID document.

83
00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:58.137
The W-2 is a US tax form

84
00:03:58.238 --> 00:04:00.340
that is issued at the end of the year.

85
00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.243
So here we would be expecting the employee_name,

86
00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:07.046
the employer_name, the year that it was issued for,

87
00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:09.315
and box one wages refers

88
00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:12.418
to the total amount that that person earned in that year.

89
00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:15.121
Here we've got one integer and one float.

90
00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:17.624
You can review the other schemas on your own time.

91
00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:21.094
Down at the bottom of the cell, notice that we are mapping

92
00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:25.098
the schemas to a shorthand name that we can use later.

93
00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:29.102
This cell uses a function provided by Landing AI

94
00:04:29.302 --> 00:04:32.105
to convert the pydantic schema to JSON.

95
00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:37.372
Recall that both JSON and Pydantic are accepted for defining your schema,

96
00:04:37.577 --> 00:04:40.380
but under the hood, we will convert Pydantic to JSON

97
00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.882
before we pass it to the extract API.

98
00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:45.518
Now we have all the components in

99
00:04:45.518 --> 00:04:48.187
place to do the parsing and the categorization.

100
00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:49.689
Let's take a look at the code

101
00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:51.190
in this cell before we execute it.

102
00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:55.161
We're going to be processing all of the documents in the input_folder.

103
00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:57.563
We'll be sending them to the parse API.

104
00:04:57.563 --> 00:05:00.366
We'll be using dpt-2-latest.

105
00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:03.169
And then this parameter is new.

106
00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:07.874
We're actually asking for a markdown response for each individual page

107
00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:10.176
rather than for the document as a whole.

108
00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:14.347
So for example, if the investment statement had 11 pages,

109
00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:17.150
we would actually get back 11 different markdowns.

110
00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:21.354
The reason for that is that in order to identify the document,

111
00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:23.556
we often only need the first page.

112
00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.925
So the identification step will just

113
00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:28.561
use the first page of the markdown.

114
00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:32.365
Here in step two, we're calling the extract API.

115
00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:35.568
We're using the doc type JSON that we defined earlier

116
00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:37.970
and we're sending the first page markdown.

117
00:05:38.371 --> 00:05:40.373
So, let's go ahead and do that now.

118
00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:42.775
The way that I've written this code,

119
00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:45.078
it's actually sending each document in series,

120
00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:48.381
but there are many options for parallelizing this workload

121
00:05:48.481 --> 00:05:50.783
that will make the entire process go faster.

122
00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.753
We can look at the results as they come back.

123
00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:57.323
UploadC has been identified as a bank statement.

124
00:05:57.824 --> 00:06:00.727
UploadA is an investment statement.

125
00:06:01.427 --> 00:06:03.329
E looks to be the W-2.

126
00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:07.033
UploadD is the identification document,

127
00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:10.236
and B is the pay stub. Fantastic.

128
00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:14.774
At this point, we have markdown corresponding to all five documents,

129
00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:17.076
and we know what type it is.

130
00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:19.879
So we're ready to apply the correct schema

131
00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:21.881
to the correct type of document

132
00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:24.484
and extract those key value pairs.

133
00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:27.854
Let's take a look at this cell before we execute it.

134
00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:31.457
First we create a dictionary to store the extraction results.

135
00:06:31.758 --> 00:06:34.427
Then for each document, we want to look

136
00:06:34.427 --> 00:06:37.463
up the appropriate schema that goes along with it

137
00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:39.165
based on its document type.

138
00:06:39.565 --> 00:06:41.868
Then we'll send the correct schema along

139
00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:44.137
with the markdown to the extract API.

140
00:06:44.804 --> 00:06:48.007
Finally, we'll store all of the results as they come back,

141
00:06:48.307 --> 00:06:52.111
focusing on the extractions themselves and the extraction metadata,

142
00:06:52.311 --> 00:06:53.880
where we'll find the visual groundings.

143
00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.715
Let's go ahead and do that now.

144
00:06:56.015 --> 00:07:00.920
Among all this output, recall that uploadD was the identification document.

145
00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:04.357
It was a passport issued by the country of Japan

146
00:07:04.557 --> 00:07:07.660
with an issue date March 24th, 2025.

147
00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.063
And recall that uploadE was the tax form

148
00:07:11.764 --> 00:07:13.966
John Doe who earned

149
00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.536
two thousand three hundred and twenty three dollars

150
00:07:16.736 --> 00:07:19.839
in the year 2024. This is all looking good.

151
00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:22.875
The top part of the output has the extractions themselves,

152
00:07:23.376 --> 00:07:26.379
and below you start to see the extraction metadata

153
00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:29.182
where you have these long chunk IDs,

154
00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:32.385
which we covered in lesson one to kind of understand

155
00:07:32.685 --> 00:07:35.855
how to connect the chunk ID back to the original document.

156
00:07:36.322 --> 00:07:39.125
Let's do some visualizations of those parsing results

157
00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:41.727
with the bounding boxes overlaid.

158
00:07:41.727 --> 00:07:44.630
This is very similar to what we did in lesson one.

159
00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.966
Here we're actually going to save each

160
00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:49.335
of the five as an annotated image

161
00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:51.604
and then we'll actually view those images.

162
00:07:51.604 --> 00:07:55.608
Here we have the annotated bank statement, which was upload C.

163
00:07:55.608 --> 00:07:58.244
See several text chunks, two major table

164
00:07:58.244 --> 00:08:01.214
chunks, and all of the cell level grounding.

165
00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:03.649
And let's look at one more.

166
00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.352
Here's the W-2 statement.

167
00:08:06.452 --> 00:08:09.355
You can see the year is nice and prominent,

168
00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:12.658
and the wages up here 2323 in box one.

169
00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:15.695
If you're building a document processing system,

170
00:08:15.695 --> 00:08:17.697
say with human in the loop,

171
00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:20.700
it can be really helpful to draw the human's eyes

172
00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.370
to the specific fields where those extracted values were found.

173
00:08:24.704 --> 00:08:27.006
Here we loop through all of the documents again,

174
00:08:27.006 --> 00:08:31.310
but we only annotate the specific fields that appear in the schema.

175
00:08:31.911 --> 00:08:33.613
Let's take a look at uploadC,

176
00:08:33.613 --> 00:08:35.615
which should be the bank statement.

177
00:08:35.915 --> 00:08:38.117
and indeed now the eye is really drawn to

178
00:08:38.117 --> 00:08:41.020
where the name appears, where the total balance appears,

179
00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.623
and where the statement date appears.

180
00:08:43.823 --> 00:08:46.626
So going back to our scenario of being the person

181
00:08:46.626 --> 00:08:49.328
who's receiving all of these loan documents,

182
00:08:49.629 --> 00:08:52.331
we're going to want to create some sort of final summary

183
00:08:52.331 --> 00:08:55.334
that represents everything that the person has provided.

184
00:08:55.835 --> 00:08:58.638
So in this block of code, we're going to go through

185
00:08:58.638 --> 00:09:00.640
all of those previous document_extractions

186
00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:03.643
and we're going to create a table with five columns

187
00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:05.645
as a Pandas data frame.

188
00:09:05.811 --> 00:09:07.580
All right, this is a pretty nice

189
00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:09.315
summary of what the person has provided.

190
00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:12.818
We've got the folder corresponding to that applicant,

191
00:09:13.019 --> 00:09:16.522
the name of the document, the type that it was categorized as,

192
00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:18.624
the field that was requested,

193
00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:22.828
and offscreen here, the field value. Let's take a look at those,

194
00:09:23.129 --> 00:09:26.899
pretty good. This would definitely save you a ton of time

195
00:09:26.999 --> 00:09:29.702
versus opening all of these documents individually

196
00:09:29.802 --> 00:09:32.405
and visually hunting for this information

197
00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:36.008
and then typing it into some sort of user interface.

198
00:09:36.409 --> 00:09:38.911
So, as we mentioned previously,

199
00:09:38.911 --> 00:09:41.414
the names on these documents do not match.

200
00:09:41.914 --> 00:09:44.417
But that would actually be a key responsibility

201
00:09:44.417 --> 00:09:47.620
of the person receiving all of these documents.

202
00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:51.424
They need to check that these assets actually belong to the person

203
00:09:51.924 --> 00:09:54.427
who applied for the loan, or that this identification

204
00:09:54.427 --> 00:09:57.930
actually belongs to the person who applied for the loan,

205
00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:01.233
and you haven't just uploaded your next-door neighbor's documents.

206
00:10:01.734 --> 00:10:04.370
So, here's a nice example of validation

207
00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:08.541
to check whether the name fields across all of these documents match

208
00:10:08.541 --> 00:10:10.943
and quickly surface if they do not.

209
00:10:11.544 --> 00:10:13.512
You can imagine now sending a trigger

210
00:10:13.512 --> 00:10:15.915
or an alert email back to the applicant

211
00:10:16.215 --> 00:10:18.517
saying, these documents don't match,

212
00:10:18.718 --> 00:10:22.355
please check your work. Here's another validation example.

213
00:10:22.855 --> 00:10:26.826
Typically when applying for a loan, you need to provide recent documents.

214
00:10:27.326 --> 00:10:29.695
And so it may be appropriate to actually check

215
00:10:29.695 --> 00:10:32.331
that all of these documents are from the same year.

216
00:10:32.832 --> 00:10:35.334
The rest of this code really just focuses

217
00:10:35.334 --> 00:10:38.137
on pulling the year out of other formatted dates.

218
00:10:38.571 --> 00:10:40.773
And again, because they're demonstration documents,

219
00:10:41.073 --> 00:10:43.376
there's some old ones that snuck in here.

220
00:10:43.776 --> 00:10:45.878
They are definitely not all from the current year.

221
00:10:46.579 --> 00:10:48.080
So, you're probably getting the hang of

222
00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.582
this, but just to round it out,

223
00:10:49.782 --> 00:10:51.784
let's sum all of the bank balances

224
00:10:52.084 --> 00:10:55.087
and all the investment balances to understand this person's

225
00:10:55.388 --> 00:10:57.490
total assets. In our example,

226
00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:01.060
there was just one bank statement and one investment statement.

227
00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:04.263
But imagine if that person had their assets spread all over

228
00:11:04.463 --> 00:11:07.266
and they had uploaded 10 different bank statements.

229
00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:09.835
This would at least bring together all

230
00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:11.971
of the assets into one final number.

231
00:11:12.171 --> 00:11:15.374
So that brings us to the end of exercise three.

232
00:11:15.875 --> 00:11:18.177
In this exercise, you have authenticated

233
00:11:18.177 --> 00:11:20.179
with the ADE API,

